---
import '@fontsource/instrument-serif/400.css';
import '@fontsource/instrument-serif/400-italic.css';
import '@fontsource/ibm-plex-sans/400.css';
import '@fontsource/ibm-plex-sans/500.css';
import '@fontsource/ibm-plex-sans/600.css';
import '@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/400.css';
import '@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/500.css';
import BaseHead from '../../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import ExtractionTool from '../../components/coffee/ExtractionTool.svelte';
import { ZONES, SOURCES, EY_BY_METHOD, METHODS } from '../../lib/coffee/index.ts';

const title = 'Extraction Map: Where Your Cup Sits Between Sour and Bitter';
const description = 'Describe how your coffee tastes and feels, see where it lands on the strength and extraction map, then pull grind, temperature, time and ratio levers to watch it move toward the sweet spot.';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={title} description={description} />
		<link rel="alternate" type="application/rss+xml" title="JayArr Coffee: Deep Brew research" href="/deep-brew/rss.xml" />
		<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
			'@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebApplication', name: 'Extraction Map', url: 'https://jayarr.coffee/deep-brew/extraction/', description,
			applicationCategory: 'UtilityApplication', operatingSystem: 'Any', offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' }, publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'JayArr Coffee', url: 'https://jayarr.coffee' },
		})}></script>
	</head>
	<body class="cf-page db-dark">
		<Header />
		<main>
			<section class="cf-intro">
				<a href="/deep-brew/" class="cf-back">← Deep Brew</a>
				<p class="cf-kicker">Deep Brew · Extraction Map</p>
				<h1>Where does your cup sit <em>between sour and bitter?</em></h1>
				<p class="cf-lede">Slide to describe the taste and the body, or enter a refractometer reading. Your cup lands on the brewing control chart, the map baristas use to talk about strength and extraction. Then pull a lever and watch it move.</p>
			</section>
			<section class="cf-tool" aria-label="Extraction Map"><ExtractionTool client:load /></section>

			<article class="cf-body">
				<h2>Two different dials: strength and extraction</h2>
				<p><strong>Extraction</strong> is how much of the coffee's soluble material ended up in the water, as a share of the dry grounds. Too little and the cup tends to taste sour and hollow; too much and it tends to turn bitter and drying. The brewing control chart grew out of E. E. Lockhart's work in the 1950s.</p>
				<p><strong>Strength</strong> is the concentration of dissolved coffee in the drink, measured as total dissolved solids (TDS), and it is set mostly by the ratio of coffee to water. This map uses the SCA's March 2019 chart as its reference: 1.15–1.45% TDS and 18–22% extraction. Earlier Golden Cup guidance used 1.15–1.35% TDS. These ranges help compare brews; your preferred strength can fall outside them.</p>
				<p>Later UC Davis research found that different groups of coffee drinkers preferred different brewing conditions. Taste your cup before deciding what to change: a higher strength does not, by itself, mean over-extraction.</p>
				<p>Because they're separate, a cup can be strong and sour, or weak and bitter. That's why the map has two axes and nine zones.</p>

				<h2>The nine zones</h2>
				<ul>
					{Object.values(ZONES).map((z) => <li><strong>{z.label}</strong> tends to taste {z.tastes.toLowerCase()}. Move: {z.moves.join('; ').toLowerCase()}.</li>)}
				</ul>

				<h2>Typical extraction by brew method</h2>
				<p>Published measurements put each method in a typical band; the map is centred on the sweet spot rather than any one method.</p>
				<ul>
					{METHODS.map((m) => <li>{m.label}: about {EY_BY_METHOD[m.id].min}–{EY_BY_METHOD[m.id].max}%</li>)}
				</ul>
				<h2>How to calculate extraction yield</h2>
				<p>With a refractometer reading, extraction is simple arithmetic: <strong>extraction yield (%) = drink weight × TDS (%) ÷ coffee dose</strong>. An 18 g espresso dose that gives a 36 g shot at 10% TDS has extracted 3.6 g of coffee, which is 20%. A 20 g pour-over that gives 300 g of coffee at 1.3% TDS has extracted 3.9 g, or 19.5%.</p>
				<p>For immersion brews (French press, AeroPress, cold brew), use all the water you added instead of the drink weight, because the liquid left in the grounds is as strong as your cup. That simple version reads slightly low. Take an immersion reading before you dilute: the grounds hold back some of the dissolved coffee, so the formula can't use a diluted cup. For pour-over and espresso, if you dilute afterwards (an americano, bypass water), take the weight and the reading at the same point: the dissolved coffee doesn't change when you add water.</p>
				<p>A refractometer is the only way to measure TDS at home. Without one, taste is a good guide, and the map is a way to reason about it: a hypothesis, not a reading.</p>

				<h2>Sources</h2>
				<ul class="cf-sources">
					{['sca-chart', 'sca-golden-cup', 'cotter-2021', 'gagne', 'rao', 'easto', 'synthesis'].map((id) => SOURCES[id]).map((s) => <li>{s.author}, {s.url ? <a href={s.url} rel="noopener"><cite>{s.title}</cite></a> : <cite>{s.title}</cite>}{s.year ? ` (${s.year})` : ''}{s.where ? `: ${s.where}` : ''}.</li>)}
				</ul>
			</article>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style is:global>.cf-page { background: var(--t-paper, #f3ede3); }</style>
<style>
	main { color: var(--t-ink, #2a1d15); }
	.cf-intro { max-width: 1180px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 120px 24px 22px; }
	.cf-back { font: 500 12px/1 'IBM Plex Mono', monospace; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--t-ink-3, #75655a); text-decoration: none; }
	.cf-kicker { font: 500 12px/1 'IBM Plex Mono', monospace; letter-spacing: .14em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--t-crema-deep, #8f5d22); margin: 26px 0 0; }
	h1 { font: 400 clamp(40px, 6vw, 68px)/.98 'Instrument Serif', Georgia, serif; letter-spacing: -.01em; margin: 10px 0 12px; }
	h1 em { font-style: italic; color: var(--t-coffee, #7a4e30); }
	.cf-lede { font: 400 17px/1.5 'IBM Plex Sans', system-ui, sans-serif; color: var(--t-ink-2, #5b4a3d); max-width: 62ch; margin: 0; }
	.cf-tool { max-width: 1180px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 8px 24px 40px; }
	.cf-body { max-width: 760px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 24px 24px 72px; font: 400 17px/1.7 'IBM Plex Sans', system-ui, sans-serif; }
	.cf-body h2 { font: 400 34px/1.1 'Instrument Serif', Georgia, serif; margin: 44px 0 12px; }
	.cf-sources { font-size: 14.5px; color: var(--t-ink-2, #5b4a3d); }
	@media (max-width: 640px) { .cf-intro { padding: 96px 16px 18px; } .cf-tool { padding: 4px 12px 32px; } .cf-body { padding: 16px 16px 56px; } }
</style>
